# EVERY CLAIM FILED AS A POINT, BESIDE WHO IT SAYS OWNS THE EDGE. One row per point claim,
# `violates` where the answer is that the bound is where the measurements fell.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ THE RULE IS THE SCHEMA'S OWN TWO SENTENCES PUT BESIDE EACH OTHER.
#    `boundOrigin absent/reason = none` is documented as: "NOTHING SETS IT. The bound is where
#    the MEASUREMENTS FELL, not where a rule put them, and there is no lever to look for."
#    A point value has no range, so nothing fell anywhere. The sentence describes a spread the
#    claim does not have, and it is the answer a receiver would act on -- go measure again and
#    the bound will move -- against a figure filed as though measuring again would change
#    nothing.
#
# ⭐⭐ AND THE SCHEMA ALREADY NAMES THE RIGHT ANSWER, IN THE ENTRY DIRECTLY BELOW IT.
#    `notApplicable` is "the quantity has no author to ask about: AN OBSERVED COUNT, a derived
#    residual". An exact observation is precisely that. `derived` is the other honest answer,
#    and it is what the great majority of point claims here give: the figure is stated in a
#    sibling element -- `Nameplate/amountOrigin`, `LumpyQuantum/origin`, and on an elimination
#    `asrt:against` plus `asrt:between` -- which is what licenses filing it without a width. So
#    this rule does not forbid an exact claim. It asks that the exactness have an author, and
#    the enum has a member for every honest one.
#
# ⭐⭐ THE CORPUS CONTAINED ITS OWN CONTROL, WHICH IS WHY THE FIRST RUN COULD BE ACTED ON.
#    Seventeen `pm:jagged/pm:draw` claims are filed. Sixteen are points and all sixteen
#    answered `none`; the ONE with a spread answers `derived`, under a comment that reads "a
#    point draw converts into a range for the same reason the nameplate does: the cards drawn
#    are EXACT, the hours they were drawn for are not." In the single place a draw is not a
#    point, the author writes down why the others are. The rule imposes no reading; it recites
#    one the documents already make somewhere else.
#    ⭐ Look for that shape before acting on any first run: the sibling that BREAKS the pattern
#      is the author's own control, and it is worth more than the count of the ones that fit.
#
# ⛔⛔ THE THIRD CORNER OF A SQUARE, AND THE PAIR IS WHY IT IS VISIBLE AT ALL. Two rules
#    already cross a claim's width against `narrowsWhen`: a point must file `notApplicable`, a
#    range must not. This crosses the same width against `boundOrigin`, the other half of the
#    required pair. Neither column alone can see it -- `none` is one of the two commonest
#    boundOrigin answers and unremarkable on its own, and the width is unremarkable on its own.
#
# ⛔ WHAT WOULD REFUTE IT: a quantity that is genuinely exact, genuinely unowned, and genuinely
#   not an observed count. The schema's own gloss on `none` argues no such thing exists, since
#   an unowned bound is by construction one that measurement placed.
-- pm:Claim/pm:boundOrigin with pm:absent/reason = none on a claim where low = high.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT c.filing, c.layer,
           c.origin_absent IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'none' AS violates,
           format('%s claim %s: %s %s exactly, and nothing is said to set it',
                  c.owns, c.seq, c.mode, c.unit) AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(epistemics/point_claims.sqlc)
    ) c
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'bound_fell_with_no_range'
